# LLMD extension
lang: typescript

// Spec for this extension, written in its own format. Format rules: @skills/llmd/SKILL.md

## [x] Current state

@src/refs.ts
  - findRefs(text: string) -> Ref[]
    - skip lines inside fenced code blocks
    - match @path not preceded by a word char, trim a trailing sentence dot
  - projectOf(file: string) -> string | undefined
    - the folder that holds the nearest .llmd/ folder above the file, or undefined

@src/outline.ts
  : OutlineNode
    - kind: 'heading' | 'unit' | 'type' | 'method' | 'field'
    - name: string
    - detail: string
    - line: number
    - endLine: number
    - children: OutlineNode[]
  - parseOutline(text: string) -> OutlineNode[]
    - skip lines inside fenced code blocks
    - headings nest by level; units go under the nearest heading
    - types go under their unit; methods and fields under their type, or the unit when there is no type
    - lines indented under a method are logic, not symbols
    - endLine is the last line that belongs to the node, so the breadcrumbs follow the cursor
    - a heading's [ ] or [x] box is not part of its name; a done step's detail is "done"
    - a "- : Name" line is a type, like ": Name"
  - openSteps(text: string) -> { title: string; line: number }[]
    - every "##" heading from parseOutline that isn't done, with its title and line
  - export the UNIT, TYPE, METHOD and FIELD patterns, so the fold keys lines the same way the outline does
  - stepSection(text: string, title: string) -> { line: number; done: boolean; lines: string[] } | undefined
    - the step's heading line, its tick, and its lines down to the next # or ## heading, without trailing blank lines
    - undefined once the step is gone, which after a run means it was folded

@src/symbols.ts
  : Declared
    - kind: 'type' | 'unit'
    - line: number
    - members: Map<string, number>
  : Call
    - name: string
    - member: string
    - line: number
    - start: number
    - end: number
  - indexSymbols(nodes: OutlineNode[]) -> Map<string, Declared>
    - every type by its name, every unit by its file name without extension, so src/refs.ts is "refs"
    - members are the methods, fields and types directly under it, mapped to their line
    - units with the same path share their members
    - a type wins over a unit with the same name, so type book in book.go is a type
  - findCalls(text: string) -> Call[]
    - every Name.member outside fenced code blocks and outside @ and + paths
    - start and end are the columns of the whole Name.member
  - builtinTypes(text: string) -> string[]
    - the everyday built-in types for the spec's "lang:" line, or every language's when it has none
  - inTypePosition(line: string, col: number) -> boolean
    - after "- name:" in a field, or after the name in "- name(...)"
  - typeSpans(text: string, nodes: OutlineNode[]) -> { line: number; start: number; end: number }[]
    - built-in and declared type names in field types and method signatures; skip qualified names like time.Time
  : Claim
    - path: string
    - name: string
    - line: number
  - claims(nodes: OutlineNode[]) -> Claim[]
    - every type and method declared under an @ unit of a done step, with the unit's path and the declaring line
    - + units are skipped: in a done step they should have become @ already
  - symbolName(name: string) -> string
    - a code symbol's plain name: "Add(double, double)", "(*Book).Save" and "List<T>" become "Add", "Save" and "List"
  // decided: types and methods only
  // fields are risky: Python attributes and Go embedded fields often aren't document symbols
  - closest(name: string, candidates: Iterable<string>) -> string | undefined
    - the candidate a typo most likely meant, ignoring case: at most 1 edit away for names up to 4 letters, 2 for longer ones

@src/extension.ts
  - activate(context: vscode.ExtensionContext)
    - warn on @refs that don't exist, using refs.findRefs
    - make @refs to files Ctrl+clickable
    - complete files and folders after @ and /, one folder level at a time
    - dim [x] methods and their logic lines, using doneLines
    - show outline.parseOutline as document symbols, using toSymbols
    - register findDefinition, completeNames and callProblems next to the other providers and warnings
    - resolve @paths from refs.projectOf when the spec is in .llmd/, else from the workspace folder as before
    - show a "▶ Implement" code lens on every openSteps step
    - on Enter on an empty "- " line, outdent one level and keep the "- "; at the outermost level, remove the bullet
    - color symbols.typeSpans as semantic "type" tokens, with semantic highlighting on for llmd
    - right after @ or a line-start +, offer every file and folder in the project, skipping .git, node_modules and build output
    - after a "/", keep walking one folder level at a time
    - add driftProblems to the spec's warnings
    - rerun it when the spec opens and whenever any file is saved
    - no drift warnings for specs in .llmd/archive/: they describe the code as it was
    - a "Plan" code lens next to "▶ Implement" on every open step, running the hidden llmd.plan command
    - Implement and Plan run run.runStep on the step
    - a step with a run gets a "Run" code lens back to it, "Working…" while the agent works
    - an "Accept" code lens on each ticked step besides Current state
  - doneLines(text: string) -> number[]
    - lines of [x] methods plus the deeper-indented lines under them
    - also every line of a done step, down to the next heading of the same or a higher level
    - headings inside fenced code blocks don't count
  - toSymbols(nodes: OutlineNode[], doc: vscode.TextDocument) -> vscode.DocumentSymbol[]
    - heading, unit, type, method, field become String, File, Class, Method and Field symbols
    - method detail is its signature, like "(dir string) -> error"; a + unit's detail is "new file"
    - an empty "## " heading is named "…", since a symbol needs a name
  - findDefinition(doc: vscode.TextDocument, pos: vscode.Position) -> vscode.Location | undefined
    - on the member part of Name.member, go to the member; on a declared name, go to its declaration
  - completeNames(doc: vscode.TextDocument, pos: vscode.Position) -> vscode.CompletionItem[]
    - after "Name." offer that name's members
    - otherwise offer every declared name: types as classes, units as files
    - nothing inside @ and + paths
    - in a type position, offer declared types first, then symbols.builtinTypes; ":" also triggers it
  - callProblems(doc: vscode.TextDocument) -> vscode.Diagnostic[]
    // unit names aren't checked: prose like "extension.ts" would look like a call
    - warn only when the member is a likely typo of a declared one, and suggest it: "Debouncer has no member 'Pussh'. Did you mean 'Push'?"
    // inherited members like DbContext.Database and file names like BookService.cs in prose aren't typos
  - enter(editor: vscode.TextEditor)
    - an empty "* " line steps out like an empty "- ", keeping its "* "
    - an empty "! " line steps out like an empty "- ", keeping its "! "
  - driftProblems(doc: vscode.TextDocument) -> Promise<vscode.Diagnostic[]>
    - group symbols.claims by path; for each file, get its symbols with vscode.executeDocumentSymbolProvider
    - flatten the symbol tree to names, without parameter lists or receivers, so "Add(double, double)" and "(*Book).Save" become "Add" and "Save"
    - warn "<path> has no '<name>'" on the declaring line when the name isn't there
    - no warnings for a file whose language has no symbol provider: no answer isn't the same as a missing symbol
  // decided: names only; language servers format parameters too differently to compare
  // decided: on open and on save, not while typing
  // after the Snapshot step, done steps are folded away, so this checks Current state
  ! with llmd.agentCommand set to a fake agent, Implement opens the step's run in the spec's column, streams in the reply, and keeps the step as it was
  ! a message resumes the step's session, and another step starts its own

@language-configuration.json
  - on Enter after a unit, type or method line, indent one level and start the line with "- "
  - on Enter after any other "- " line, start a "- " at the same indent
  - on Enter after a "- : Name" line, indent one level and start the line with "- "
  - on Enter after a "* " line, start a "* " at the same indent
  - on Enter after a "! " line, start a "! " at the same indent

@syntaxes/llmd.tmLanguage.json
  - color @path and +path unit lines like file names
  - color ": Name" lines like type names
  - color "? ..." lines like warnings and "// ..." lines like comments
  - markdown headings, code blocks and inline markdown for everything else
  // decided: LLMD lines win over markdown; tables, quotes and numbered lists lose markdown colors
  - color "- : Name" like ": Name"
  - color "* ..." lines with a scope of their own, so they stand out from logic and ? lines
  - color "! ..." lines: the "!" like a keyword, the text with a scope of its own; @refs and inline markdown still work inside

@src/test.ts
  - check findRefs, parseOutline, indexSymbols and findCalls on small specs
  - check step names and the "done" detail in the outline
  - check projectOf with / and \ paths, nested folders, and a spec outside .llmd/
  - check "- : Name" in the outline, typeSpans, builtinTypes and inTypePosition
  - check fold: new unit, merged method, + to @, ~ removals, decided lines, open steps untouched
  - check claims: done vs open steps, @ vs + units, types and methods
  - check closest: a typo, a case slip, nothing close, short names
  - check fold keeps "!" lines under a method and drops step-level ones to the archive
  - check openSteps, and stepPrompt for both modes
  - check agentArgs with and without a session, and render on a recorded stream: text, tool calls, a denial, an error
  - check stepSection on an open step, a done one and a folded one, and that render keeps the edited file
  - check agentArgs without the snapshot allowance, snapshotName, foldInto into a new and an existing snapshot, and takeNamed
  - check allowRules on a compound command, a pipe and another tool, and that a denial carries its rules

@src/e2e.ts
  - in a real VS Code: warnings, links, @ completion, colors, Enter, dimming and the outline
  - go to definition, "Name." completion and a typo warning
  - check that a done step and its subsections are dimmed, and an open step isn't
  - check that Implement shows on open ## steps only
  - check Enter after a logic line, then stepping out twice from an empty bullet
  - check "- : Name" coloring, type completion, semantic tokens and @ finding a nested file
  - check "* " coloring and Enter after a "* " line
  - a done step listing a function missing from a TypeScript file gets a warning; one that exists doesn't
  - an open step listing a missing function gets no warning
  - the typo warning suggests the declared member; an undeclared member that isn't a typo doesn't warn
  - check that Plan shows next to Implement on open steps only
  - check "! " coloring and Enter after a "! " line
  - Implement with a fake agent that ticks the step opens its run in the spec's column and streams the reply; a message resumes it, and another step gets its own session
  - Accept shows on done steps; the run test's spec is a draft, its fake agent names a snapshot, and Accept folds into it
  - in a git repository, the fake agent's edit shows as a one-line diff; Allow adds its rule next to what the settings had
  - Accept on a step the agent didn't tick asks it to finish the step, and leaves the step alone when it doesn't

@skills/llmd/SKILL.md
  - "## [ ] Title" is a step and "## [x] Title" is a done step
  - "implement the next step" means the first unticked step from the top; touch only what it lists
  - a ? blocks only the step it sits in; text above the first step is context for every step
  - tick the step once all of it is built and builds; don't add [x] to methods
  - in a git repo, offer to commit a finished step with its title as the message
  - when describing existing code as llmd, put it all in one "## [x] Existing code" step
  - look for specs in .llmd/ when the user names none; ask if there are several; write new specs there
  - "## Title" is an open step, same as "## [ ] Title"; ticking makes it "## [x] Title"
  - "- : Name" and folder units in the syntax table
  - before a step, skim the ## step titles and ticks of the other specs in .llmd/
  - open another spec in full only when a search for this step's paths finds it; raise conflicting open steps as questions
  - after ticking a step, run "llmd snapshot <spec>"; if llmd isn't installed, say so and leave the step ticked
  - "~" lines in a step mean remove; describing existing code writes "## [x] Current state"
  // decided: fold right after each tick
  - "* line" in the syntax table: a requirement the agent designs itself, under a unit, a type, a method or the step
  - for "*" lines, decide instead of asking; ask only at a real fork, like two designs with different trade-offs
  - before ticking, replace each "*" line with the "-" lines it built: units, types, signatures and short logic
    - the user's wording survives in the archive, so nothing is lost
  - this replacement is one more spec edit the agent may make on its own
  - the report lists the decisions it made for "*" lines, so the user can review the design along with the diff
  // decided: a "*" line at step level lets the agent choose the files; the report lists them
  // decided: build, then report the decisions; no design approval first
  - before ticking, make sure the step, once folded, describes the code as it really is
    - add "~" lines for anything in Current state the step's code changed or removed
    - a line with parentheses is read as a method; write a record's or class's data as fields
  - adding those "~" lines is one more spec edit the agent may make on its own
  - "plan the step" reads like implementing, then discusses: what's unclear, what's missing, what clashes with the code or other specs
  - it proposes concrete spec edits (? lines, new lines, new steps) and makes them only once the user agrees; it never writes code
  - "! line" in the syntax table: a check that must hold once the step is built, under a unit, type, method or the step
  - before ticking, verify every "!" line in the step: as a test when the project has tests, else by running or reading the code
  - don't tick while a check fails; report it instead
  - the report lists each check and how it was verified
  - planning suggests "!" lines for what the step should prove
  // decided: the agent never rewrites "!" lines; they're the user's, unlike "*" lines
  - specs are one per feature, plus app.llmd for shared setup like startup, the db context and auth
  - a shared file appears in every spec that adds to it, each listing only its own feature's part
  - a step goes in the spec of the feature it's for, even when it touches another feature's files
  - with several specs and none named, use the one the request points to ("the next book step"), else ask
  - describing existing code writes one spec per feature plus app.llmd, each with its own Current state; one spec is fine when there are no clear features
  - drafts and snapshots: a draft holds one new step; the snapshots are each feature's Current state and the step's context
  - before ticking, add "snapshot: <feature>" under the step's heading: the snapshot it extends, or a new name
  - llmd snapshot folds a named step into its snapshot; when the prompt says the user accepts, don't fold
  - "snapshot:" in the syntax table, and one more spec edit the agent may make on its own

@README.md
  - a landing page: pitch, a short example, install, quick start, contributing and license
  - how to install the extension, the skill and the CLI
  - links to the guide by absolute GitHub URL, so they also work in the packaged extension
  - the extension bullet says Implement turns the editor into the step's run
  - the quick start writes the step with New feature and ends with Accept

@docs/guide.md
  - the in-depth how-to, moved out of the README, with a contents list at the top
  - specs go in .llmd/, with a tip on gitignoring it per repo or globally
  - steps without [ ], and prompts that don't need the spec's path
  - describe the new Enter behaviour
  - "- : Name", folder units, @ search and type help
  - say the agent skims the other specs, and asks about conflicting open steps
  - install the CLI with npm link, the snapshot, ~ lines, and redoing a step as a new step
  - the suggested AGENTS.md line points at Current state as the architecture; Claude Code gets a CLAUDE.md that imports it
  - how to write a spec, with every line type and an example
  - how to work with the agent from a spec: implement, answer ? questions, review, snapshot and commit
  - explain steps: how to write them, "implement the next step", ticking, and a commit per step
  - "* line" in the line types table, and a section on handing decisions to the agent, with an example mixing "*" and "-"
  - that the agent replaces "*" lines with what it built before the step is folded
  - drift warnings under Editor help, and that they need the language's extension installed
  - in Known limits: only names are checked, not signatures or logic
  - typo warnings only fire for likely typos, and say what was meant
  - the archive isn't drift-checked
  - written for any agent: "the agent", not "Claude"
  - "! line" in the line types table, and a short section on checks with an example
  - the review list says the agent verified every "!" line
  - a "One spec per feature" section with a book example and the shared-file rule
  - the AGENTS.md line points at the Current state of every spec in .llmd/
  - "llmd snapshot" without a name folds every spec; describe writes one spec per feature plus app.llmd
  - New feature, drafts, snapshots and Open feature in Start a project; Accept under Runs; snapshot: in the line types; snapshot lines under The snapshot
  - a section on runs: the live step, Changes from git, the conversation, Allow and Continue, the status, Accept, Edit spec, one session per step, auto mode; claude only for now

@src/agent.ts
  - stepPrompt(mode: 'implement' | 'plan', step: string, file: string) -> string
    - implement: 'implement the step "<step>" in <file>'
    - plan: 'plan the step "<step>" in <file>' plus: discuss it, ask about anything unclear, suggest what to add or change in the spec, write no code
  - agentArgs(prompt: string, session?: string) -> string[]
    - "-p --output-format stream-json --verbose", "--resume <session>" when there is one, then "--" and the prompt
  : Part
    - kind: 'text' | 'tool' | 'error'
    - text: string
    - file: string
    // only on a tool call that changed a file
    - allow: string[]
    // only on a refused tool call: the rules that would let it run
  - render(event: any, root: string) -> Part[]
    - an assistant's text as markdown, and a tool call as one line, like "Edit cmd/main.go" or "Bash go build ./..."
    - the result's permission denials as "Not allowed: ...", and its errors
    - nothing for thinking, hooks and tool results
    - a tool call that edits a file keeps the file's path, for the run's Changes
    - a permission denial carries agent.allowRules for its tool call
  - allowRules(name: string, input: any) -> string[]
    - one rule per command in a shell line, in Claude Code's own format: "Bash(go build *)" for "go build ./... && ..."
    - a second word counts only for tools with subcommands, like go, git, npm or dotnet; any other tool is allowed by its name

@package.json
  - the llmd.agentCommand setting and the hidden llmd.implement command
  - use images/file.svg as the llmd language icon, for light and dark themes
  - images/icon.png as the extension's icon in the Marketplace, Open VSX and the Extensions view
  - a "bin" entry for llmd, so "npm link" puts it on the PATH
  - "prepack" compiles, so the release tarball is prebuilt and installing it runs no scripts
  - the LLMD: New feature and LLMD: Open feature commands, the hidden llmd.run and llmd.accept, and activation when a run's tab is restored
  - files.exclude hides .llmd/drafts by default
  - llmd.agentCommand is "claude --permission-mode auto" by default, and says how Allow works

@.npmignore
  - the npm package is only the CLI: out/cli.js, refs.js, snapshot.js and outline.js
  - engines.vscode and @types/vscode at 1.93, the first version with terminal shell integration events

@images/file.svg
  - a checklist glyph, one ticked box and one open, in purple

@images/icon.svg
  - the logo: the file.svg glyph, scaled up on a dark rounded tile so it reads on light and dark backgrounds
  - rendered to images/icon.png at 256px, since the Marketplace and READMEs packaged by vsce don't accept SVG

@src/snapshot.ts
  : Line
    - text: string
    - children: Line[]
  - fold(text: string) -> { text: string; folded: string[] }
    - merge every done ## step, top to bottom, into "## [x] Current state", then remove the step
    - create Current state above the first step when there's none
    - ### headings inside a step are flattened; its top-level // notes and prose are dropped, except // decided: lines
    - folded is the removed steps' text, for the archive
  - merge(into: Line[], from: Line[])
    - match lines by key: a unit by path, a type, method or field by name, anything else by its text
    - a new line is appended with its children; + units become @
    - a matching symbol takes the step's signature, then its children merge the same way
    - a "~" line removes its match: "- ~Stop()", "~@old.go", "- ~walk dir"
    // a rename is a ~ of the old name plus the new one
  - key(line: string) -> string
  // decided: folded steps are appended to .llmd/archive/<spec>.llmd
  // decided: a step's logic lines are appended, duplicates skipped; old ones stay until a ~ removes them
  - snapshotName(step: string[]) -> string | undefined
    - the feature a step's "snapshot: <feature>" line names
  - foldInto(snapshot: string | undefined, step: string[], title: string, lang?: string) -> string
    - fold one step into a snapshot's Current state, as done whatever its box says
    - a new snapshot starts with "# <title>", the lang: line and an empty Current state
  - takeNamed(text: string) -> { text: string; steps: { name: string; lines: string[] }[] }
    - take the done steps that name a snapshot out of a spec, for them to fold into it

@src/cli.ts
  - main(args: string[])
    - "llmd snapshot [spec]" folds the spec in place, appends the folded steps to .llmd/archive/<spec>.llmd and prints their titles
    - warn when a folded step still has "*" lines: the agent didn't replace them, so Current state holds prose
    - without a spec, fold every .llmd file in .llmd/, and say "Nothing to fold" only when none had done steps
    ! "llmd snapshot" with two specs that each have a done step folds both and archives each under its own name
    - without a spec, also fold the drafts in .llmd/drafts/
  - snapshot(spec: string) -> boolean
    - fold one spec, archive its folded steps and print their titles; false when it had none
    - a done step with a snapshot: line folds into .llmd/snapshots/<name>.llmd instead, and a draft with no steps left is deleted

// decided: claude only for now, through "claude -p" with stream-json output; other agents can come later

// decided: no terminals; Plan and Implement of a step share its session, and the Plan prompt keeps it from writing code

// decided: each ## step is its own chat, like a migration; once it's built it folds into Current state, the snapshot

// decided: the run opens in the spec's editor group, in front of the spec; Edit spec flips back

@src/run.ts
  - rootOf(uri: vscode.Uri) -> vscode.Uri
    - the folder holding .llmd/, else the workspace folder, else the spec's folder
  : Run
    - spec: vscode.Uri
    - step: string
    - messages: Message[]
    - before: string[]
    - last: string[]
    - drafting: boolean
    - committed: string
  - openRun(spec: vscode.Uri, step: string) -> Run
    - open the step's run as a webview tab in the spec editor's column, in front of the spec, or show it again
  - runStep(mode: 'implement' | 'plan', spec: vscode.Uri, step: string)
    - agent.stepPrompt, into the step's run
    - an implement prompt tells the agent to stop at the tick: you accept the step
  - send(run: Run, text: string)
    - your message, plus which step it's about and the lines selected in the spec's editor
    - every message tells the agent not to fold the step or run llmd snapshot
  - ask(run: Run, text: string, prompt: string)
    - save the spec and keep the step's lines from before its first run, then run llmd.agentCommand with agent.agentArgs at the project root
    - each step has its own claude session: its first message starts it, the next ones resume it
    - stream agent.render's parts into the run, markdown through VS Code's own markdown.api.render
    - keep each step's session id and its run in the workspace state; forget the session when resuming fails
    - one agent per step at a time; Stop ends it
    - when a run ends, read its changes from git before it counts as finished
    - at the step's first run, keep its git base: snapshot of the working tree
  - editSpec(run: Run)
    - back to the spec's editor, in the run's column, at the step
  - initRuns(context: vscode.ExtensionContext)
    - keep a run's step live as the spec changes, in the editor or on disk
    - the LLMD: New feature and LLMD: Open feature commands, the hidden llmd.run and llmd.accept, and a serializer so a run's tab comes back after a reload
    - a spec deleted or created refreshes its runs too
  - newFeature()
    - ask for a name, then write a draft .llmd/drafts/<name>.llmd holding the project's lang: line and "## <name>", and open it under the step
  - openFeature()
    - pick a draft to open again, start a new feature, or discard a draft
  - accept(run: Run)
    - fold the step, read from disk, into the snapshot its snapshot: line names, or one you pick, with snapshot.foldInto
    - take the step out of its spec; a draft with no steps left is closed and deleted
    - a step the agent didn't tick: ask it to make the step describe what it built, add its snapshot: line and tick it, then fold
    - if it still isn't ticked after that, say so and leave the step where it is
    - a step that's gone: accepted if it was folded, else say there's nothing to accept
  ! with a fake agent that ticks the step and names a snapshot, Accept writes .llmd/snapshots/<name>.llmd and takes the step out of the draft
  - updateDiffs(run: Run)
    - every file git sees changed between the base and a snapshot of now, .llmd files left out
    - each file's old and new lines highlighted by its extension
    - .claude/settings.local.json left out too
  - allow(run: Run, rules: string[]) -> Promise<boolean>
    - add the rules to the project's .claude/settings.local.json, keeping what's there; leave a file it can't read alone
  : Diff
    - file: string
    - status: string
    - old: string[]
    - new: string[]
  - snapshot(cwd: string) -> Promise<string | undefined>
    - git add -A into .git/llmd-index, then git write-tree: the working tree as a tree, ignored files left out
  - highlight(lines: string[], lang: string) -> Promise<string[]>
    - one fenced block through markdown.api.render, split back into lines, spans that run over a line closed and reopened
    - plain past 2000 lines, or when the renderer isn't there
  - openChanges(run: Run, file?: string)
    - one file in vscode.diff, all of them in vscode.changes; the old side through the llmd-base scheme, git show <base>:<file>
  ! a file a command wrote shows up, an untracked file from before the run doesn't, and its code has hljs spans
  - writeMessage(run: Run) -> Promise<string | undefined>
    - claude -p with the step, its diff and the last 10 commit subjects for style; the reply without code fences goes into the commit box
  - commit(run: Run, message: string)
    - git add -A and git commit -- on the step's paths only; a deleted file git never tracked is left out; a failure says why
    - keep "<hash> <subject>" on the run
  - nextSteps(root: vscode.Uri) -> Promise<{ spec, step, where }[]>
    - the open steps in .llmd/drafts/ and then .llmd/, at most 8
  ! Commit takes a.ts, b.ts and the snapshot, not notes.txt, the draft or the local Claude settings
  - specText(uri: vscode.Uri) -> Promise<string | undefined>
    - the editor's text only while it has unsaved edits, else the file on disk
  - post(run: Run)
    - a ticked step that left its spec is accepted, into the snapshot its snapshot: line names, else its own spec
  ! Accept on a ticked step whose draft was deleted under its open editor accepts it

@media/run.js
  - draw the run: a bar with the step, its status and what you can do next, then the live step and the conversation
  - the step drawn like the editor, with line numbers and a line diff since the run started: added lines green, removed ones struck
  - runs of more than three tool calls fold into one "N actions" line
  - it opens with the step alone, like the editor it replaces, then slides into two columns
  - Enter sends; while the agent works, Enter keeps your message and the button stops it
  - Changes: one foldable file each, with its added and removed lines and git's diff, line numbers and hunk headers
  - outside a git repository, only the edited files' names
  - Allow on each refused command, "✓ Allowed" once it is, and Continue with what you allowed in the last message
  - "✓ Accept" in the bar once the agent has had a turn, "into <snapshot>" when it named one; an accepted run says where it went
  - Changes is a section you fold, with Open all
  - diff rows use the highlighted lines
  - Open shows the file's changes in the diff editor
  - an accepted run: the Changes section moves into the right column in a view transition, every file unfolded
  - above it, the commit box with Write with Claude and Commit, Cmd/Ctrl+Enter commits; "✓ Committed <hash> <subject>" after
  - the review starts when you click Accept, also while Claude finishes the step; Commit waits until it's accepted
  - above the step, Next: each open step opens its run, and New feature

@media/run.css
  - the step and the conversation side by side, stacked in narrow tabs, in VS Code's theme colors
  - diff rows like the editor's diff colors, tabs four wide
  - the review column, the commit box and the Next list; no animation with reduced motion
  - Accept is choreographed in a view transition: the conversation slides out, the changes lift out of the step's column and open up as the main view, then the commit box and Next rise in

// decided: drafts in .llmd/drafts/, hidden from the explorer and search; snapshots in .llmd/snapshots/<feature>.llmd

// decided: the agent picks the snapshot with a "snapshot: <feature>" line when it ticks; you accept, the agent doesn't fold

// decided: nothing is archived for an accepted step; git keeps its text

// decided: claude runs in auto permission mode by default; acceptEdits is the fallback for plans without it

// decided: the diff is against the working tree as the step's first run found it, so earlier uncommitted work stays out

// decided: the base is a git tree of the whole working tree, untracked files in, written through an index of llmd's own

// decided: a run without a base, from before Changes or before git init, counts from the last commit

// decided: the code is highlighted by markdown.api.render, the preview's own highlight.js, and colored by its highlight.css

// decided: the move is the browser's own view transition on the Changes section; no transition, no animation

// decided: a commit takes only the step's files, its snapshot, and its spec when it isn't a draft, so other work stays out

// decided: Claude writes the message in a one-off headless run from the step and its diff, not in the step's chat

// decided: .claude/settings.local.json is your local permissions, so it's left out of Changes and the commit

// decided: a ticked step, or one with a snapshot: line, that leaves its spec counts as accepted, however it was folded

## Fold in VS Code

// The extension folds done steps itself, with the same code as llmd snapshot, so VS Code users don't need the CLI.

@src/cli.ts
  - snapshot(spec: string) -> string[]
    - ~fold one spec, archive its folded steps and print their titles; false when it had none
    - fold one spec and archive its folded steps; return what to tell the user, nothing when there was nothing to fold
    - exported, so the extension folds the same way
  - main(args: string[])
    - print the lines snapshot returns

@.vscodeignore
  - ship out/cli.js in the extension too, for Fold

@src/extension.ts
  - activate(context: vscode.ExtensionContext)
    - a "Fold" code lens on each done step besides Current state
  - foldSpec(uri: vscode.Uri)
    - save the spec, run cli.snapshot on it and show what it says
  ! Fold on a done step moves it into Current state and appends it to .llmd/archive/, exactly like llmd snapshot

@media/run.js
  - a step that's done but not folded gets a Fold button in its run's bar

@src/e2e.ts
  - Fold shows on done steps only, and folding leaves just Current state

@docs/guide.md
  - the Fold button under The snapshot, and that VS Code needs no CLI for it

## Snapshots that keep up with the code

// In a real Go project, Current state stopped matching the code after a few steps:
// - routes() shows twice: under ": application", and again at file level, where later steps added their routes
// - "- (app *application) getIndex()" isn't read as a method, so it can't merge, show in the outline or get drift warnings
// - one step removed main() with ~ and listed it again with only its new lines, so Current state lost the logger and the listen
// - a ~ only reaches its own spec, so a shared file's removed line lives on in the other spec that listed it

@src/outline.ts
  - METHOD also reads a Go receiver: "- (app *application) getIndex()" is the method getIndex
  - parseOutline(text: string) -> OutlineNode[]
    - a method with a receiver goes under that type when its unit declares it, else under the unit

@src/snapshot.ts
  - merge(into: Line[], from: Line[])
    - a type, method or field matches the same name anywhere in its unit, not just at the same level, when only one has that name
  - fold(text: string) -> { text: string; folded: string[]; warnings: string[] }
    - warn when a step removes a method with ~ and lists it again: Current state then keeps only that step's lines for it
  - prune(text: string, steps: string[]) -> string
    - apply only the ~ lines of steps folded from another spec to this spec's Current state

@src/cli.ts
  - snapshot(spec: string) -> string[]
    - prune the other specs in .llmd/ with the folded steps, since the code lost those lines whichever spec lists them
    - pass on fold's warnings
  ! folding a step in book.llmd with "- ~print the result" under a shared file removes that line from loan.llmd's Current state too

@skills/llmd/SKILL.md
  - to change a method's logic, ~ the lines that changed and add the new ones; ~ the method itself only to delete it
  - a Go method can be written with its receiver, "- (a *app) routes()", or under its type
  - while planning, suggest turning - lines that ask for something, like "Lets improve it with a better layout", into * lines, so they're replaced by what was built

@src/test.ts
  - check a receiver method in the outline and the fold, a file-level method merging into its type, prune, and the remove-and-list-again warning

@docs/guide.md
  - Go receivers in the line types table
  - a ~ reaches every spec, and the warning for removing a method and listing it again

## The spec knows the code

// Names in a spec point at the real code, through the language's own symbol provider, the one drift warnings already use.
// Built names are colored like code, hovering shows their code, and Ctrl+click opens it. Names that aren't built yet stay plain.

@src/symbols.ts
  - indexSymbols(nodes: OutlineNode[]) -> Map<string, Declared>
    - in a go spec, a file unit is also known by its folder, its package: internal/store/sqlite.go is "store" too, so store.GetRecords resolves

@src/extension.ts
  - symbolsOf(uri: vscode.Uri) -> Promise<Map<string, vscode.DocumentSymbol> | undefined>
    - each name with its symbol, so the spec knows where it is and what kind it is; the first wins when names repeat
  - codeSymbol(doc: vscode.TextDocument, pos: vscode.Position) -> Promise<{ uri: vscode.Uri; symbol: vscode.DocumentSymbol } | undefined>
    - on a type, method or field line under an @ unit: that name in the unit's file
    - on a Name.member call: the member in the file where the spec declares it
  - activate(context: vscode.ExtensionContext)
    - Ctrl+click on a type, method or field line opens its file at that symbol; Name.member still jumps to its line in the spec
    - hovering a name the code has shows its first lines of code, colored as its language, with its path and line
    - names the code has get a semantic token by their kind there, like class, interface, function, method or property
    - refresh those tokens when any file is saved
    // a name in an open step can already exist, when the step changes existing code, so this covers every step
  ! a method under an @ unit that its file has is colored and opens the file at it; one the file lacks stays plain

@src/e2e.ts
  - in the drift fixture: the built method opens store.ts at it, its hover shows its code, and it gets a token; the planned one gets none

@docs/guide.md
  - under Editor help: built names colored like code, hover and Ctrl+click into the code, and that this needs the language's extension, like drift warnings
